window.docContents[52].resolve({"/Algebraic-Graph-Theory/#section-the-hypercube-and-the-hamming-graph":{"contents":"When we first introduced the hypercube in Hypercube we gave two\nother constructions. One of these was the Hamming graph\nhypercubeHamming n: the graph on Fin n → Bool in which two vertices are adjacent\niff their Hamming distance, computed by Mathlib's hammingDist for Pi-types, is one.\nHaving identified the graph distance in Q[n] with the Hamming distance in\nDistance In The Hypercube, we are now in a position to prove that\nthis construction agrees with the one we adopted, i.e. Q[n] ≃g hypercubeHamming n.\n\nFirst we need to identify the two vertex types, i.e. give an equiv\nHypercubeVert n ≃ (Fin n → Bool). We build this by recursion on n. The base case\nholds since both types have exactly one element. In the inductive step we send\n(v, b) to the function whose first n coordinates are those of v and whose last\ncoordinate is b. Mathlib's Fin.snoc, which appends an element at the end of a\ntuple, does exactly this (and Fin.snocEquiv is Fin.snoc as an equiv).\n\n#check @Fin.snoc      -- append an element at the end of an n-tuple\n#check Fin.snocEquiv  -- Fin.snoc as an equiv\n\n\n/-- The equiv between the two vertex types of the hypercube, HypercubeVert n and\nFin n → Bool, sending (v, b) to Fin.snoc v b, so b is the last coordinate. -/\ndef HypercubeVert.equivPi : (n : ℕ) → HypercubeVert n ≃ (Fin n → Bool)\n  | 0 => Equiv.ofUnique Unit _\n  | n + 1 => ((equivPi n).prodCongr (Equiv.refl Bool)).trans <|\n      (Equiv.prodComm _ _).trans (Fin.snocEquiv fun _ => Bool)\n\n\nUnfolding the equiv one step is definitionally Fin.snoc, and marking this as\n@[simp] lets simp compute with equivPi recursively.\n\n@[simp]\nlemma HypercubeVert.equivPi_succ_apply {n : ℕ} (v : HypercubeVert n) (b : Bool) :\n    equivPi (n + 1) (v, b) = Fin.snoc (equivPi n v) b := rfl\n\n\nTo transfer distances along the equiv we need to know how Mathlib's hammingDist\ninteracts with Fin.snoc: appending a coordinate to each of two vertices increases\ntheir Hamming distance by one exactly when the appended coordinates differ. (Note how\nthis mirrors the recursion in HypercubeVert.hammingDist.) To prove this we write\nthe Hamming distance as a sum of indicators, using Finset.card_filter, and split\noff the last coordinate with Fin.sum_univ_castSucc.\n\nlemma hammingDist_snoc {n : ℕ} {β : Type*} [DecidableEq β] (x y : Fin n → β)\n    (a b : β) :\n    hammingDist (Fin.snoc x a : Fin (n + 1) → β) (Fin.snoc y b) =\n      hammingDist x y + if a = b then 0 else 1 := by\n  simp only [hammingDist, Finset.card_filter, Fin.sum_univ_castSucc,\n    Fin.snoc_castSucc, Fin.snoc_last, ne_eq, ite_not]\n\n\nThe Hamming distance of HypercubeVert n vertices agrees with the Hamming distance\nof the corresponding functions Fin n → Bool, by induction on n.\n\n@[simp]\nlemma HypercubeVert.hammingDist_equivPi {n : ℕ} (u v : HypercubeVert n) :\n    _root_.hammingDist (equivPi n u) (equivPi n v) = u.hammingDist v := by\n  induction n with\n  | zero => rfl\n  | succ n ih =>\n    obtain ⟨u, b⟩ := u\n    obtain ⟨v, c⟩ := v\n    simp [hammingDist_snoc, ih]\n\n\nAdjacency in the Hamming graph is Hamming distance one, by definition.\n\n@[simp]\nlemma hypercubeHamming_adj {n : ℕ} {x y : Fin n → Bool} :\n    (hypercubeHamming n).Adj x y ↔ hammingDist x y = 1 := Iff.rfl\n\n\nWe can now give the graph isomorphism: the underlying equiv is\nHypercubeVert.equivPi, and it respects adjacency since both adjacency relations say\nexactly \"Hamming distance one\".\n\n/-- The hypercube Q[n] (the box product construction) is isomorphic to the Hamming\ngraph construction hypercubeHamming n on Fin n → Bool. -/\ndef hypercubeIsoHamming (n : ℕ) : Q[n] ≃g hypercubeHamming n where\n  toEquiv := HypercubeVert.equivPi n\n  map_rel_iff' := by\n    simp [hypercube_adj_iff_hammingDist_eq_one]\n\n\nThis isomorphism is a bridge between the two constructions: results proved for\nQ[n] by induction transport to hypercubeHamming n, while statements that need\nthe coordinates of a vertex, such as identifying the automorphism group of the\nhypercube, are more naturally proved for hypercubeHamming n and transported back.\n\nTransporting edist_eq_hammingDist along hypercubeIsoHamming shows that the graph\ndistance in the Hamming graph is exactly the Hamming distance.\n\nlemma hypercubeHamming_edist_eq {n : ℕ} (x y : Fin n → Bool) :\n    (hypercubeHamming n).edist x y = hammingDist x y := by\n  obtain ⟨u, rfl⟩ := (HypercubeVert.equivPi n).surjective x\n  obtain ⟨v, rfl⟩ := (HypercubeVert.equivPi n).surjective y\n  exact ((hypercubeIsoHamming n).edist_eq u v).trans (by simp [edist_eq_hammingDist])\n\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean\u0009Algebraic Graph Theory","header":"12.5. The Hypercube and the Hamming Graph","id":"/Algebraic-Graph-Theory/#section-the-hypercube-and-the-hamming-graph"},"/Distance/#section-distance-in-box-products":{"contents":"The distance in a box product is the sum of the distances in its two factors: a walk\nin G □ H moves in one coordinate at a time, so it projects to a walk in each\nfactor, and conversely walks in the two factors can be run one after the other.\nMathlib records this as edist_boxProd. Together with the distance in a complete\ngraph, edist_top, it will let us compute distances in the hypercube, which we\ndefined as an iterated box product in Hypercube.\n\n#check edist_boxProd  -- (G □ H).edist x y = G.edist x.1 y.1 + H.edist x.2 y.2\n#check edist_top      -- (⊤ : SimpleGraph V).edist u v = if u = v then 0 else 1\n\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean\u0009Distance\u0009Graph Distance","header":"11.1.3. Distance in Box Products","id":"/Distance/#section-distance-in-box-products"},"/Distance/#section-the-cage-bound-for-odd-girth":{"contents":"If the minimum degree is at least δ + 1 and 2 * i + 4 is less than the girth,\nthen a vertex of Lᵢ₊₁ has at least δ neighbours in Lᵢ₊₂ (all its neighbours\nexcept one in Lᵢ), and distinct vertices of Lᵢ₊₁ have no common neighbours there.\nBy the second counting lemma (mul_card_le_card_of_adj), we have\nδ * #Lᵢ₊₁ ≤ #Lᵢ₊₂.\n\nlemma mul_card_layer_le [Finite V] [G.LocallyFinite] {δ : ℕ}\n    (h_deg : ∀ v, δ + 1 ≤ G.degree v) (r : V) {i : ℕ} (hg : 2 * i + 4 < G.egirth) :\n    δ * #(G.layer r (i + 1)) ≤ #(G.layer r (i + 2)) := by\n  classical\n  refine mul_card_le_card_of_adj (G := G) (fun u hu => ?_)\n    (fun u hu x hx v hv huv hxv => ?_)\n  · obtain ⟨w, hwu, hw⟩ := exists_adj_edist_eq i (by simpa using hu)\n    -- every neighbour of u other than w lies in Lᵢ₊₂\n    have := degree_le_degIn_add_one (G.layer r (i + 2)) w\n        fun x hx hxw => by\n      have := edist_eq_add_two_of_adj (by simpa using hu) hw hwu hx hxw\n        (hg.trans_le' (by norm_cast; grind))\n      rw [mem_layer]\n      exact_mod_cast this\n    grind\n  · simp only [mem_layer] at hu hx hv\n    exact eq_of_adj_of_adj hu hx (by rw [hv]; rfl) huv hxv\n      (by push_cast; convert hg using 1; ring)\n\n\nBy induction #Lᵢ₊₁ ≥ (δ + 1) * δ ^ i, provided that 2 * i + 2 is less than the\ngirth.\n\nlemma card_layer_ge [Finite V] [G.LocallyFinite] {δ : ℕ}\n    (h_deg : ∀ v, δ + 1 ≤ G.degree v) (r : V) {i : ℕ} (hg : 2 * i + 2 < G.egirth) :\n    (δ + 1) * δ ^ i ≤ #(G.layer r (i + 1)) := by\n  induction i with\n  | zero => simpa [layer_one] using h_deg r\n  | succ i ih =>\n    calc (δ + 1) * δ ^ (i + 1) = δ * ((δ + 1) * δ ^ i) := by ring\n      _ ≤ δ * #(G.layer r (i + 1)) := by\n          gcongr\n          exact ih (hg.trans_le' (by norm_cast; grind))\n      _ ≤ #(G.layer r (i + 2)) :=\n          mul_card_layer_le h_deg r (by push_cast at hg; convert hg using 1; ring)\n\n\nIf the girth is at least 2 * k + 1 this holds for every i < k, and since the\nlayers are disjoint, L₀, …, Lₖ contain at least 1 + (δ + 1) * ∑ i < k, δ ^ i\nvertices. The layers around any vertex r already contain this many vertices, so G\nneed not be connected; we only need V to be nonempty. We state the girth\nhypothesis with egirth, so it also covers acyclic graphs (whose egirth is ⊤).\n\ntheorem cage_bound_odd [Finite V] (G : SimpleGraph V) [G.LocallyFinite]\n    [Nonempty V] (δ k : ℕ) (h_deg : ∀ v, δ + 1 ≤ G.degree v)\n    (h_girth : 2 * k + 1 ≤ G.egirth) :\n    1 + (δ + 1) * ∑ i ∈ range k, δ ^ i ≤ Nat.card V := by\n  classical\n  have := Fintype.ofFinite V\n  obtain ⟨r⟩ := ‹Nonempty V›\n  calc 1 + (δ + 1) * ∑ i ∈ range k, δ ^ i\n      = ∑ i ∈ range k, (δ + 1) * δ ^ i + 1 := by rw [mul_sum, add_comm]\n    _ ≤ ∑ i ∈ range k, #(G.layer r (i + 1)) + #(G.layer r 0) := by\n        gcongr with i hi\n        · exact card_layer_ge h_deg r (h_girth.trans_lt' (by norm_cast; grind))\n        · simp [layer_zero]\n    _ = ∑ i ∈ range (k + 1), #(G.layer r i) :=\n        (sum_range_succ' (fun i => #(G.layer r i)) k).symm\n    _ = #((range (k + 1)).biUnion (G.layer r)) :=\n        (card_biUnion fun i _ j _ hij => disjoint_layer hij).symm\n    _ ≤ Nat.card V := Nat.card_eq_fintype_card (α := V) ▸ card_le_univ _\n\n\nInformally, the cage bound holds trivially for infinite graphs, so it might seem\nthat the hypothesis [Finite V] is unnecessary. But in Lean it is essential, because\nof the junk value 0 of Nat.card for an infinite type. For example, the infinite\npath pathNat₁ is acyclic and has minimum degree 1, so without [Finite V] the\ncage bound with δ = 0 and k = 1 would claim that 2 ≤ Nat.card ℕ = 0.\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean\u0009Distance\u0009Moore and Cage Bounds","header":"11.4.6. The Cage Bound for Odd Girth","id":"/Distance/#section-the-cage-bound-for-odd-girth"}});